# QAOA — Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm
# ===================================================
#
# QAOA solves combinatorial optimization problems on quantum hardware.
# The canonical problem: MaxCut.
#
# Given a graph G = (V, E), partition the nodes into two sets S and S̄
# to maximize the number of edges CUT between S and S̄.
#
# This is NP-hard classically. QAOA provides a quantum approach with
# provable approximation guarantees:
#   p=1: ratio ≥ 0.6924 (proven by Farhi, Goldstone, Gutmann 2014)
#   p→∞: converges to exact solution
#
# Aether syntax — the cleanest way to express this problem:
#
#   graph G:
#       nodes N
#       edge i j
#
#   infer G using qaoa(layers=p, shots=1024)
#
# No circuit code. No gate decomposition. No Pauli string manipulation.
# Just the graph. Aether compiles it to QAOA automatically.
#
# Compare with Qiskit: ~80 lines. Aether: ~10 lines.

# ── Triangle graph ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The simplest non-trivial MaxCut problem.
# 3 nodes, 3 edges — MaxCut = 2 (one node isolated).
#
#     0
#    / \
#   1 — 2

graph Triangle:
    nodes 3
    edge 0 1
    edge 1 2
    edge 0 2

infer Triangle using qaoa(layers=1, shots=2048)

# ── Butterfly graph ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 4 nodes, 5 edges — MaxCut = 4.
# Two triangles sharing a vertex.
#
#   0   3
#    \ /|
#     2 |
#    / \|
#   1   4

graph Butterfly:
    nodes 5
    edge 0 2
    edge 1 2
    edge 2 3
    edge 2 4
    edge 3 4

infer Butterfly using qaoa(layers=2, shots=2048)

# ── Petersen-inspired 6-node graph ────────────────────────────────────
#
# 6 nodes, 9 edges — a denser graph that challenges p=1 QAOA.
# MaxCut requires p≥2 for high-quality solutions.
#
#   0 — 1 — 2
#   |   |   |
#   3 — 4 — 5

graph Grid2x3:
    nodes 6
    edge 0 1
    edge 1 2
    edge 3 4
    edge 4 5
    edge 0 3
    edge 1 4
    edge 2 5

infer Grid2x3 using qaoa(layers=2, shots=2048)
